    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures), and the audit line is emitted:\n\n```\nINFO  AUDIT - owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-04\n```\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe create pipeline (`owners.POST.yml`) already ends with `save \u2192 respond`, and by the time `SaveOwner` runs the record has its `customerCode` (from `AssignCustomerCode`) and `registrationDate` (from `BuildOwner`), with the `id` assigned by the save itself. So the one place where all three values exist together on success is right after the `save()` call.\n\nI made a single edit to `SaveOwner.java`: added a static `AUDIT` slf4j logger (matching the existing logger idiom in `GeneralExceptionHandler`) and one `audit.info(...)` line after the save carrying the owner id, customerCode and registrationDate.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\n- **One file touched**, so the file multiplier is 1.\n- **One function changed** \u2014 `SaveOwner.service` \u2014 whose class has no other methods (`WMC_other = 0 \u2192 1`) and whose cyclomatic complexity stays 1 (the added line is straight-line, no branches). The change is ~3 lines.\n\nThat gives roughly `max(1,0)\u00b71\u00b73 \u00b7 1 file \u2248 3`. Introducing a new pipeline step class instead would have meant editing the YAML plus a new class = 2 files, a strictly higher cost, for the same behaviour. The added logging is a simple, non-branching statement, so no method or class grew in complexity.",
